# Untitled

## Single-Crystal Transformation Engineering the Spin Change of Metal–Organic Frameworks via Cluster Deconstruction
## Introduction
Spin crossover (SCO) complexes are typical bistable materials with potential applications in data storage, switches, displays and sensors.[1–10] They can be triggered between high-spin (HS) and low-spin (LS) states under physical perturbations such as temperature, light and pressure or chemical stimuli such as guest molecules and redox substances. Since the covalent linkers between spin transition centers have been found to get strong SCO cooperativity, coordination polymers, such as one-dimensional (1D) iron(II)-triazole system, two-dimensional (2D) \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{L})_{2}(\mathrm{NCX})_{2}\right]\) (L=bispyridyl ligands, X=S, Se or \(\mathrm{BH}_{2}\)) family and three-dimensional (3D) Hofmann-type \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{L})\{\mathrm{M}(\mathrm{CN})_{\mathrm{m}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}\right]\) metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have been extensively studied.[11–17] Meanwhile, MOFs built from metal-cluster secondary building units have been developed greatly during the past decade, which exhibit rich structural tunability, interesting topologies, extra-large cavities and unique phys
icochemical properties.[18–22] However, SCO MOFs containing clusters as nodes have not been reported until now.
In addition to direct synthesis of MOFs, post-synthetic modification (PSM) is an effective method to tailor the framework to get new functionalities.[23–27] In the SCO field, only a few PSM cases have been reported. For example, redox PSM on the cyanometalate linkers, inverse-electron-demand Diels–Alder reaction of pillar ligands and photochemical [2+2] cycloaddition reaction had been performed to effectively tune the SCO behaviours.[28–35] Single-crystal to single-crystal (SCSC) transformation is beneficial to investigate the structural differences in the PSM process.[36–40] However, since the crystallinity is not easily retained after PSM, it is still a great challenge to explore new PSM methods for SCO materials.
Here, we report two SCO MOFs \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\{\mathrm{Ag}_{8}\mathrm{Br}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\}-\left(\mathrm{TPBA}\right)_{3}\right]\cdot9\mathrm{DMF}\) (1, \(\mathrm{TPBA}=\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N},\mathrm{N}^{\prime},\mathrm{N}^{\prime}\)-tetra(pyridin-4-yl)benzene-1,4-diamine) and \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\{\mathrm{Ag}_{8}\mathrm{I}_{6}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\}-\left(\mathrm{TPBA}\right)_{3}\right]\cdot8\mathrm{DMF}\) (2) with the urk topology, in which novel \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8}\mathrm{X}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\) serves as hexatopic building blocks. Benefiting from the rigid 3D framework constructed by tetradentate ligands and iron ions, 2 can further react with tetracyanoquinodimethan (TCNQ) anion to subtract silver halide cluster to form a new framework \(\left[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}\right]\)-\(\left(\mathrm{TPBA}\right)\)]:\(\left(\mathrm{KTCNQ}\right)\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot5.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) (3) with the nbo topology via SCSC transformation. Unsurprisingly, their magnetic and fluorescence properties are greatly altered after the cluster deconstruction. Hence, the SCO MOFs with metal cluster as building blocks are explored for the first time, which will represent a new family in the SCO field.
## Results and Discussion
Slow diffusion of \(\mathrm{K}\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}\right]\) and TPBA into \(\mathrm{FeX}_{2}\) (X=Br and I) in MeOH/DMF solution yielded yellow single crystals, which were collected and further dried at 343 K for 30 min to remove MeOH guests to form 1 and 2, respectively. The MeOH/DMF solution with LiTCNQ was added into the mother liquor containing the original single crystals of 2 for 3 days, which result in the black crystal of 3 and blue-purple powder AgTCNQ. Similarly, the SCSC transformation from 1 to 3 can also be achieved (Figure S7), which is ignored to prevent repetition.
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SC-XRD) analyses reveal that 1 and 2 crystallize in the trigonal R3c space group at 120 and 290 K with similar 3D framework structure expect different halogen atoms,[41] in which a unique
centrosymmetric \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{X}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\) cluster anion serves as secondary building unit (Figure 1). Here, we only use 2 to describe their structural characteristics. The asymmetrical unit contains one sixth of the formula (Figure S8 and S9). The center of the \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{X}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\) cluster anion and the center of the TPBA ligand locate in the inversion center. Two Ag1 and six \(\mathrm{Ag} 2\) ions are arranged in a distorted cube and the six \(\mu_{4}-\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions cap each face of the Ag cube yielding a \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{I}_{6}\right]^{2+}\) cage with a near-regular rhombic dodecahedron geometry (Figure 1 and Figure S10). All the \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)ions are tetrahedrally coordinated. Ag1 ion is bound to three \(\mu_{4}-\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions and one terminal I1 ion. Such Ag1 and I1 ions are aligned in the 3-fold rotoinversion axis, which are also along the \(c\) axis. \(\mathrm{Ag} 2\) ion is surrounded by three \(\mu_{4}-\mathrm{I}^{-}\)ions and one C atom from the \(\mathrm{CN}^{-}\)ligand. The \(\mathrm{Ag} 2 \cdots \mathrm{Ag} 2\) and \(\mathrm{Ag} 1 \cdots \mathrm{Ag} 2\) contacts are 3.419 and \(3.690 \, \mathrm{\AA}\) at \(120 \, \mathrm{K}\), respectively, which illustrate the weak argentophilic interactions (Table S4).
The \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{I}_{6}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\) cluster is linked to six \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions via the cyanide ligands, and each \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ion is surrounded by two \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{I}_{6}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\) clusters, which generates a 3D inorganic connectivity skeleton (Figure S11). The \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ion is axially coordinated by two cyanide ligands and equatorially ligated by four tetrapyridyl TPBA ligands, giving rise to the \(\left[\mathrm{FeN}_{6}\right]\) coordination environment. Each TPBA ligand utilizes all four pyridyl N atoms and acts as a planar 4-connecting node to coordinate with four \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions at the equatorial positions, which generates a 3D organic framework with M-Ligand-M connectivity (Figure S11). According to the classification made by Cheetham and Rao, the dimensionality of hybrid inorganic-organic framework materials can be represented as \(\mathrm{I}^{n} \mathrm{O}^{m}\), where \(n\) and \(m\) denote the dimensionalities of inorganic (I) and organic (O) connectivity, respectively.[42] Hence, 1 and 2 represent the unprecedented high-dimensional \(\mathrm{I}^{3} \mathrm{O}^{3}\) hybrid frameworks with voids.[43,44] In another
words, the hexatopic linkers \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{I}_{6}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}\right]^{6-}\), tetratopic TPBA ligands and octahedral \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions assemble to form 3D framework with the urk topology (Figure 2 and Figure S12 and S13). The DMF molecules in the pores are highly disordered, which were omitted by the solvent mask in OLEX2.[45]
The SCO behaviours are revealed by variable-temperature SC-XRD measurements (Table S1–S4). The average Fe–N bond lengths at \(120/290 \, \mathrm{K}\) are \(1.982/2.180 \, \mathrm{\AA}\) and \(1.985/2.174 \, \mathrm{\AA}\) for 1 and 2, respectively, which are consistent with the LS/HS states of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ion. Accordingly, the unit cell volumes in 1 and 2 increase by \(5.6\,\%\) and \(6.6\,\%\) along with the spin transition process, respectively. Meanwhile, the octahedral distortion parameters \(\Sigma\) of \(\mathrm{I}/\Sigma\) increase from \(10.3/7.1^{\circ}\) at \(120 \, \mathrm{K}\) to \(18.3/9.1^{\circ}\) at \(290 \, \mathrm{K}\) respectively, which are consistent with the normal trend of higher \(\Sigma\) at higher temperature.
Complex 3, obtained via PSM of 2 with LiTCNQ, crystallizes in the same \(R3c\) space group (Table S5). However, the lattice parameter \(c\) increases by \(4.484 \, \mathrm{\AA}\) and the unit cell volume increases by \(435 \, \mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) at \(120 \, \mathrm{K}\). The asymmetrical unit only contains a half of the formula (Figure S14). Since the \(\mathrm{Ag}^{+}\)ions react with the \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anions to form the blue-purple precipitate \(\mathrm{AgTCNQ}\) (see below), the \(\left[\mathrm{Ag}_{8} \mathrm{I}_{6}\right]\) cluster is destroyed and removed from the 3D framework (Figure 1). Next, the cyanide ligands adopt the terminal coordination mode and undergo the cyanide linkage isomerization from \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N} \equiv \mathrm{C}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{C} \equiv \mathrm{N}\). The average \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{N}\) and \(\mathrm{Fe}-\mathrm{C}\) bond lengths are \(1.925\) and \(1.801\) at \(120 \, \mathrm{K}\) (Table S6), respectively, corresponding to the LS state. The remaining TPBA ligands and Fe atoms sever as 4-connecting nodes to form an nbo type topology (Figure 2 and Figure S15 and S16). Hence, the single-crystal to single-crystal transformation from urk to nbo topology is firstly realized by the PSM method in the field of MOFs. Except one water, the other solvent molecules and KTCNQ
as the guests are highly disordered and then omitted by solvent mask in OLEX2.[42] The presence of KTCNQ is further confirmed by Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR), electron spin resonance (ESR), scanning electron microscopy (SEM), energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) and electrospray ionization mass spectrometry (ESIMS) analyses (see below).
The PSM from 2 to 3 was further revealed by IR spectra at room temperature. The cyanide stretching vibration of 2 locates at \(2130\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which is assigned to \(\nu(\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})\) in the \(\mathrm{Fe-N}\mathrm{C-Ag}\) configuration. After PSM, three peaks at 2057, 2129 and \(2176\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are observed in 3 (Figure 3a and Figure S17). The strongest peak at \(2057\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) can be assigned to the \(\nu(\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})\) for the terminal cyano group in the \(\mathrm{Fe-C}\equiv\mathrm{N}\) configuration, which is higher than \(2047\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in \(\mathrm{K}_{4}[\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]\) due to the decrease in the coordination number of cyano group.[46,47] Hence, it confirms the cyanide linkage isomerization from \(\mathrm{Fe-N}\equiv\mathrm{C}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe-C}\equiv\mathrm{N}\) after the cluster deconstruction. Reminiscent of the \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anion used as a raw reaction material, the IR band at \(2176\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) should correspond to \(\nu(\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})\) of \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anion, although the KTCNQ powder exhibits the \(\nu(\mathrm{CN})\) vibrations at 2198, 2183 and \(2168\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).[48–49] Meanwhile, it precludes the presence of the neutral TCNQ with a single sharp peak at \(2223\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\).[50] In order to explore the nature of the peak at \(2129\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), we immersed 3 in DMF for two weeks to remove the KTCNQ guest. Only one peak at \(2057\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponding to the terminal cyano group in the host framework is left, which
confirms that the two peaks at 2129 and \(2176\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) in 3 belong to the \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)guest.
The ESR measurement was further performed to examine the \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anion radical in the pores (Figure 3c and Figure S18). The ESR signals of the TPBA ligand and 2 are silent (Figure S18). Meanwhile, only a central peak with a \(g\) value of 2.0076 is observed in 3, which is similar to the ESR signal of LiTCNQ with \(g=2.0065\) and then fully confirms the presence \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anion radical. Since the framework of \([\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}(\mathrm{TPBA})]\) is neutral, there should be the presence of cations in the pores to balance the charge of the \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anions (Figure S19). The EDS mapping of 3 indicates that although LiTCNQ is used during the PSM process, the cation is \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)rather than \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\) (Figure 3e and Figure S21). The \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\)cation should come from the mother liquor of 2, where \(\mathrm{K}[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{CN})_{2}]\) is used as the starting material.
In order to outline the entire post-synthesis reaction, the by-product, that is the blue-purple powder precipitate, was further characterized by IR spectroscopy (Figure 3b and Figure S22). The \(\nu(\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{N})\) vibrations at 2197, 2181 and \(2167\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), the \(\nu(\mathbf{C}\equiv\mathbf{C})\) stretch at \(1508\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and the \(\delta(\mathrm{C}-\mathrm{H})\) bending mode at \(823\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are characteristic for the presence of \(\mathrm{AgTCNQ}\).[51] Hence, we can speculate on the whole postsynthesis reaction. When the single crystal of 2 reacts with LiTCNQ, the silver halide cluster is destroyed by \(\mathrm{TCNQ}^{-}\)anion and removed from the framework by generating the precipitate of \(\mathrm{AgTCNQ}\). The bridged cyano group becomes terminal and then the cyanide linkage isomerization from
\(\mathrm{Fe{-}N{=}C}\) to \(\mathrm{Fe{-}C\equiv N}\) is realized in the presence of solution. The \(\mathbf{K}^{+}\) cations in the mother liquor combine with the \(\mathrm{TCNQ^{-}}\) anions and enter into the channels of 3 to serve as guest molecules. Therefore, a new PSM method is realized in the SCO field.
Variable-temperature magnetic susceptibility data at \(2\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) were measured to explore the influence of PSM on the spin state. For 1, the \(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T\) value calculated by the formula with one iron unit is \(3.98\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) at \(300\,\mathrm{K}\), indicating the HS \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions. It decreases to \(0.33\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) upon cooling to \(10\,\mathrm{K}\), which suggest the almost complete SCO behaviour with \(\approx8\,\%\) of residual HS \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions at low temperature. As shown in Figure 4, the thermal SCO process of 1 is gradual. The spin transition temperature is \(243\;\mathrm{K}\) as revealed by the differential magnetic curve (Figure S23). Compared with 1, the spin transition process of 2 is more gradual, but tends to appear at higher temperature. The differential magnetic curves exhibit one peak at \(259\,\mathrm{K}\) in the cooling mode and two peaks at 245 and \(263\;\mathrm{K}\) in the warming mode, suggesting an asymmetric one-/two-step SCO behaviour (Figures S24 and S25). The SCO behaviours of 1 and 2 were further confirmed by the differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) measurement at \(10\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\). For 1, a pair of exothermic and endothermic peaks at 239 and \(242\,\mathrm{K}\) are consistent with one-step SCO behaviour from the magnetic data (Figure S26). Meanwhile, one exothermic peak at \(262\,\mathrm{K}\) and two endothermic peaks at 245 and \(273\,\mathrm{K}\) correspond to the asymmetric SCO behaviour of 2 (Figure S27). In contrast, the \(\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T\) value of 3 is only \(0.42\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{K}\,\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\) at \(300\,\mathrm{K}\), which is consistent with a radical guest while the \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions in the framework are in the LS state (Figure S28). Therefore, the spin state is directly changed and the SCO behaviour is disappeared after PSM.
Temperature-dependent fluorescence emission spectra were also investigated to explore the influence of PSM on the photoluminescence properties (Figure 5 and Figures S29–S35). Since the excitation wavelength of \(308\,\mathrm{nm}\) was used for complexes 1 and 2 (see below), the temperature-dependent fluorescence emission spectra of TPBA
ligand were also investigated under this condition. At \(90\,\mathrm{K}\), two intense emission bands at 482 and \(390\,\mathrm{nm}\) and a sharp peak at \(448\,\mathrm{nm}\) are observed for the TPBA ligand upon excitation at \(308\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S29). Upon warming, the emission intensities at \(390\,\mathrm{nm}\) gradually decrease due to thermal quenching. In contrast, the sharp peak at \(448\,\mathrm{nm}\) rapidly weakens and disappears at \(130\,\mathrm{K}\). Meanwhile, the intense band at \(482\,\mathrm{nm}\) shows a red shift to \(508\,\mathrm{nm}\) at \(130\,\mathrm{K}\) with quickly decreased intensities and eventually disappears above \(175\,\mathrm{K}\), which results in only one peak around \(390\,\mathrm{nm}\) in the higher temperature region.
For complex 1, a broad peak at \(488\,\mathrm{nm}\) with a weak peak at \(398\,\mathrm{nm}\) are observed upon excitation at \(308\,\mathrm{nm}\) and \(90\,\mathrm{K}\) (Figure 5 and Figure S21), which are slightly red-shifted with significantly weaker emission intensities as compared to those of TPBA ligand (Figure S29b). Since silver halide clusters usually exhibit interesting photoluminescence properties, the halogeno(gyano)argentite building blocks may contribute to the broad emission band.[52–54] Upon warming, the normalized emission intensities at \(488\,\mathrm{nm}\) gradually decrease, which shows different trend as compared to that of TPBA (Figure S36). Unlike the red shift of the TPBA ligand below \(130\,\mathrm{K}\), the maximum emission of 1 shows a bathochromic shift of \(18\,\mathrm{nm}\) during the SCO process (Figure 5b), suggesting the spin-state dependent fluorescence.[2,55–62]
For 2, three medium-intensity emission peaks are observed at 386, 440 and \(458\;\mathrm{nm}\), which are blue-shifted as compared to those of 1. The blue-shift from \(398\;\mathrm{nm}\) in 1 to \(386\;\mathrm{nm}\) in 2 may attribute to the decrease in the \(\pi\) conjugation system caused by the rotation of pyridyl groups in the TPBA ligand. Reminiscent of \([\mathrm{Ag}_{6}(\mathrm{CN})_{3.36}\mathrm{Br}_{5.61}]^{3-}\) and \([\mathrm{Ag}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{4}\mathrm{I}_{7}]^{3-}\) with the respective emission bands at 473 and \(448\;\mathrm{nm}\),[51] the blue-shift for the \(\mathrm{Ag-I}\) cluster may be due to the coupling of metal-to-ligand charge-transfer (MLCT) transitions. The temperature-dependent normalized emission intensities at 440 and \(458\;\mathrm{nm}\) in 2 exhibit different trends as compared to those of TPBA, which hints at the influence of the SCO process on the fluorescence properties (Figures S37 and S38). In contrast, the fluorescence is quenched in 3 and even 3 without KTCNQ, indicating that PSM can effectively regulate the photoluminescence properties.
## Conclusion
We have successfully synthesized two MOFs based on tetradentate organic ligands and \([\mathrm{Ag}_{8}\mathrm{X}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{6-}\) (\(\mathrm{X=Br}\) and I) units, which represents the first SCO MOF with metal clusters as building blocks. The tetrapopic TPBA ligands, hexatopic linkers \([\mathrm{Ag}_{8}\mathrm{X}_{8}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}]^{6-}\), and octahedral \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions assemble to form 3D frameworks with the urk topology. Further reacting with LiTCNQ, the silver halide cluster can be removed after PSM. Hence, the topology changes from urk to nbo via SCSC transformation. Accordingly, the SCO and fluorescence properties are disappeared after PSM. These findings will inspire further studies on the chemical design of new SCO MOFs containing metal clusters, and also enrich the development of PSM methods.
